.TH SPIRE 1
.SH NAME
spire, spireshop, spirespring \- a climb up a tower, and the rooms off it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B games/spire
[
.B -t
|
.B -s
[
.B -r
] ] [
.B -q
]
.PP
.B "mount {games/spireshop [" file ]}
.I dir
.br
.B "mount {games/spirespring [" file ]}
.I dir
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Spire
is a climb, in the shape that genre took: the view goes up and does not
come back down, so what has been passed is gone and falling has a price;
the world wraps left to right, so there is no edge to the tower, only a
top; there are things drifting about that must be jumped over or gone
round; and there are doors in the wall that lead somewhere with entirely
different rules.
.PP
Arrow keys or
.B wasd
move.
.BR x ,
.B .
or space jumps, held for height.
.B Up
in a doorway goes in.
.B q
or Escape quits.
.SS The doors are mounts
A room is not part of this program.
Going through a door runs a program that serves four files over Styx and
mounts what it serves on
.BR /n/room ;
the game then reads the room's name out of a file, reads what it has to
offer out of a file, and writes to a file to take something.
Coming out is an unmount, and the room's process ends when the
connection does \- which is why neither room in this tree has to
remember anything between visits.
The mount point is made by
.IR mntgen (4),
mounted on
.B /n
in the game's own name space.
.PP
Nothing in
.I spire
knows one room from another.
Which program is behind which door is
.BR /lib/tiles/spire.doors ;
the room decides what it stocks, what it charges and what it will
refuse; and the game spends what the room says it spent, not what it
thought the price was.
Adding a room means writing a server and adding a line to that file.
See
.IR roomsrv (2)
for what a room has to serve, which is little enough that both of the
rooms here are under a hundred lines with their contents in a file.
.PP
There is no fallback.
If the mount fails the door does not open and says why.
A door that opened onto something built into this program would make the
mount decoration, and a test of it would pass on the decoration.
.SS What a room can hand over
The game understands three words, and only these three:
.TP
.B jump
a longer jump, for the rest of the climb
.TP
.B shield
one blow taken for you
.TP
.B heart
another try
.PP
A room may stock any of them, at any price, in any order, or none.
A room offering anything else is not refused: it is taken, it has no
effect, and the game says so rather than pretending to know.
.SS The two rooms here
.I Spireshop
is a market: it sells from
.B /lib/tiles/shop.stock
for the coins picked up on the climb, refuses what cannot be paid for,
and refuses anything already sold.
.I Spirespring
sells nothing: it gives one draught to whoever reaches it, from
.BR /lib/tiles/spring.stock ,
and is then still until the visit ends.
Both serve on standard input and output, so either can be mounted by
hand from a shell to look at:
.IP
.EX
mount {mntgen} /n
mount {games/spireshop} /n/room
cat /n/room/wares
echo coins 100 > /n/room/ctl
echo buy jump > /n/room/ctl
cat /n/room/status
.EE
.SS The tower is a file
.B /lib/tiles/spire.map
is the tower, in the tile-map format
.IR tiles (2)
already reads: a legend, then the grid, one character a cell.
It is exactly one screen wide because the world wraps.
.B /lib/tiles/spire.art
declares the sheets, the font and the palettes and is itself a script in
.IR tiles (2)'s
command language.
Nothing about the tower or its art is compiled into the program.
.SS Other options
.B -q
starts silent.
.B -t
runs the self test and needs no window.
.B -s
plays four seconds and writes the frame to
.B spire.raw
as 24-bit BGR;
.B -r
goes through the first door first, so the picture is of a room.
.SH FILES
.TP
